---
import EnPost from '../../layouts/EnPost.astro';
import RobotAgeQuiz from '../../components/en/RobotAgeQuiz.astro';
import { QUIZ_PAGE_EN } from '../../data/robotAgeQuiz.en.mjs';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<EnPost
	title={QUIZ_PAGE_EN.title}
	description={QUIZ_PAGE_EN.description}
	updated="3 November 2026"
	faqSchema={[
		{
			question: 'Does a young child need a screen?',
			answer: 'Not before about 6 or 7, if the goal is logic without a tablet. From the middle-school years, a computer or tablet opens Scratch and then Python.',
		},
		{
			question: 'Scratch or Python?',
			answer: 'Scratch blocks from about age 8. Written Python is more realistic from 10 or 11, with an adult nearby at the start.',
		},
	]}
>
	<p set:html={QUIZ_PAGE_EN.intro.replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>').replace(/\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)]+)\)/g, '<a href="$2">$1</a>')} />
	<RobotAgeQuiz />
	<h2>Match each age band to a longer guide</h2>
	<figure>
		<img
			src={withBase('/images/models/makeblock-mbot2.webp')}
			alt="Makeblock mBot2, the example used in the table for ages 10 to 13"
			width="800"
			height="600"
		/>
	</figure>
	<p>
		The quiz points at one model. The age guides go into the build, the software (
		<strong>Scratch</strong> blocks, then <strong>Python</strong>) and what the kit actually costs.
		Euro figures on those guides are Amazon.fr readings, not a converted US or UK price.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Age</th><th>Guide</th><th>Example</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>4–6</td>
				<td>
					<a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-a-4-year-old/')}>Robot from age 4</a>
					· <a href={withBase('/robot-educatif-sans-ecran-guide/')}>Screen-free (French)</a>
				</td>
				<td>Tale-Bot, Code &amp; Go</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>7–9</td>
				<td><a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-an-8-year-old/')}>Guide for age 8</a></td>
				<td>Codey Rocky, mBot</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>10–13</td>
				<td>
					<a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-a-10-year-old/')}>Guide for age 10</a>
					· <a href={withBase('/en/mbot-vs-mbot2/')}>mBot vs mBot2</a>
				</td>
				<td>mBot2, CyberPi</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>14 and up</td>
				<td>
					<a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-a-12-year-old/')}>Guide for age 12</a>
					· <a href={withBase('/en/arduino-robot-kit-for-kids/')}>Arduino kit</a>
				</td>
				<td>ELEGOO, Raspberry Pi</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		Wider comparison:
		<a href={withBase('/en/best-programmable-robot-for-kids/')}>best programmable robots in 2026</a>
		· tight budget:
		<a href={withBase('/robot-moins-de-50-euros/')}>robots under €50 (French)</a>
		· a whole class:
		<a href={withBase('/en/class-robot-budget-calculator/')}>classroom budget calculator</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		More age guides:
		<a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-a-5-year-old/')}>age 5</a>
		· <a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-an-8-year-old/')}>age 8</a>
		· <a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-a-10-year-old/')}>age 10</a>
		· <a href={withBase('/en/best-robot-for-a-12-year-old/')}>age 12</a>.
	</p>
	<p>Some links on this page are affiliate links. The button opens Amazon so you can check today’s price.</p>
</EnPost>
